c(1, "header lines 1-8", "positioning", "Blossom Habit Tracker (Fern) (App Store ID 6770147415; subtitle 'Build habits. Grow every day') by Frances Ling (bundle com.fernhabit.app; rebranded from 'Fern' — 'Fern' in reviews 10 June, 'blossom' by 11 August; a separate listing 'Fern: Goal & Habit Tracker' ID 6757623670 is not this corpus) — 91 written reviews, 29 storefronts, 10 Jun → 4 Sep 2026 (87 days), mean 4.956, extracted 8 Sep 2026, analysed 12 Sep 2026; business model (review-derived): free download, no subscription, no paywall, no free-tier habit cap, no ads, a voluntary in-app tip as the only payment surface; zero reviewers report paying; founder-led TikTok acquisition", "fully free, unlimited habits, no ads, voluntary tip", "praise", "91 reviews; mean 4.956", "none", "corpus-level fact", "app-specific", ["14166656933","14415759525"])
c(2, "§How to read this; §1.1–1.5", "data-caveat", "Method: all 91 reviews read individually in original language (English plus Spanish and Taglish); 52 hand-curated themes, mean 3.40 per review (max 10); keyword searches afterwards only as an audit (three discrepancies, one genuine boundary correction applied); validated 0 unknown IDs, 0 intra-theme duplicates, 0 unassigned, 0 empty themes; reconciliation exact against 29 by_country files and manifest (5:89 / 4:0 / 3:2 / 2:0 / 1:0; mean 4.95604); _state.json 66 storefronts queried, all complete, 29 with reviews, 37 zero (jp, nl, se, dk, fi, ch, sg, nz, ae, eg, id, il, vn, pe, cl, ec) — real absence for a 3-month-old listing; no duplicates; vote_count and vote_sum zero on all 91, is_edited false on all 91; HTML entities unescaped; body median 140 characters, mean 172.6, min 2 (two emoji), max 1,030; denominator 91 — one review 1.10%, every theme lands 'meaningful' or higher, so raw count and storefront spread are the honest ranking signals; no review ID transcribed by hand; widest error bars on simplicity vs ease of use, free vs no-cap, and comparative_best (a bare 'Best habit app ever!!!' counted, a bare 'Amazing!!!' not)", "n/a", "n/a", "91 (100%)", "none", "method statement", "app-specific", ["14200381141","14389332832","14241077207","14450651496"])
c(3, "§Nine warnings 1–4; §1.6", "data-caveat", "Warnings: tiny corpus with a degenerate rating distribution — 89 of 91 at 5★ (97.80%), 2 at 3★, not a single 4★, 2★ or 1★ — no conventional ratings analysis possible; 87 days long — a launch-window corpus, no long-run trend; measures first impressions, not retention (median body 140 characters, 25 of 91 at ≤60 characters, two had only just downloaded, nobody has used it more than 87 days) — every praise theme means 'what makes people adopt', not 'what makes people stay'; the absence of negative reviews is a finding about the corpus, not proof of quality — comparable habit-tracker corpora run 60–75% five-star; explanations: the app removes the category's biggest anger source (the paywall), and it is too young for anyone to hit a renewal, data loss or year boundary; 14 of 91 (15.38%) volunteered a request or defect inside a 5★ — the only quality signal", "n/a", "n/a", "89/91 5★; 87 days", "none", "corpus-level fact", "generalisable", ["14385677429","14424966134"])
c(4, "§Nine warnings 5; §1.4 table (verbatim)", "data-caveat", "No review burst, vote manipulation or solicited reviews: max 4 reviews on any day (9 Jul, 14 Jul, 25 Jul, 1 Sep), 91 reviews over 56 distinct days; month table (verbatim): June 12 (5.000), July 41 (4.951), August 31 (5.000), September to the 4th 7 (4.714); weekly volume 2 · 4 · 2 · 6 · 10 · 11 · 14 · 7 · 6 · 11 · 5 · 5 · 8, peak 20–26 July (14) — the review curve of an app in its first growth phase; only 3 low-info reviews (3.30%), an unusually low contentless rate that supports organic positivity", "n/a", "n/a", table("## 1.4 Date range and shape", 0), "none", "table", "app-specific", ["14165946824","14166656933","14510494540"])
c(5, "§Nine warnings 6; Executive summary 8; §8.2.4", "tactic", "TikTok is a named, measurable acquisition channel — and the only one anyone names: 8 reviewers (8.79%, mean 5.000) attribute discovery to TikTok, several to the creator personally — 'I found them on tiktok and immediately had to download' (AU); 'I saw the creator talking about her app on TikTok and I'm so happy!!' (CA); 'thanks to that tik tok girl i forgot her name lol' (JO); 'I heard of it from TikTok when you advertised it' (SA); no reviewer names App Store search, a friend or an ad; 'I can't believe how few reviews there are but how beautiful the app is' (PH) — acquisition is single-channel and founder-dependent: a concentration risk and a confound (a creator's own audience is predisposed to rate favourably — part of the 4.956 is audience selection); TikTok attribution fell 6/45 (13.3%) → 2/46 (4.3%) between halves — diversification or attribution fatigue, indistinguishable", "founder TikTok videos", "5★-burst", "8 (8.79%, 5.000); 13.3% → 4.3%", "do", "high-priority (n=8)", "generalisable", ["14217435167","14328125592","14309581453","14305573901","14276142725"], side="inflates the mean via audience selection", cond="solo-founder launch")
c(6, "§Nine warnings 7–9; §2.3", "data-caveat", "Only the US is largest at n=26 (28.57%), half the threshold — no standalone country claim; classification hand-curated; the App Store listing and fernhabit.com egress-blocked, only search summaries (low confidence) — they corroborate free, unlimited habits, no subscription, no ads, no paywall, dark mode, custom week start, widget, and the Fern → Blossom rebrand; ADHD positioning and the tip were not found in external summaries (corpus-only)", "n/a", "n/a", "US 26 (28.57%)", "none", "method statement", "app-specific", [])

# EXECUTIVE SUMMARY
c(7, "Executive summary 1; §3.3.1 table (verbatim)", "product-rule", "'It is free and it does not cap my habits' is not a feature of this product — it is the product: 52 of 91 (57.14%, mean 4.962) praise the monetisation model itself — free 42 (46.15%), no habit cap or premium gating 14 (15.38%), no ads 8 (8.79%), competitor paywall contrast 6 (6.59%), no earned points 1 — a larger share than praise for simplicity, ease or design: 'Finding a app that you don't have to pay for is like finding diamonds' (CA); 'For this app to be free with everything you get feels like a crime' (US); 'No hidden payments or subscriptions' (GB); 'there's no hidden ~premium~ features' (US); a Brazilian review names all four sub-themes ('all the options were in some way or another paid stuff… no adds, or a bad free version so you are obligated to sign up for a premium version'); stable month to month (50% / 46% / 42% / 57%) — not a novelty effect — 'the pricing decision is doing the work that features normally do'; 'in most habit-tracker corpora the monetization family is a negative aggregate… Here it is a positive aggregate of the same size'", "fully free, no cap", "praise", table("### 3.3.1 The monetization model as the product", 0), "product-rule", "high-priority", "generalisable", ["14417648063","14393257431","14507313209","14401530018","14245487733","14282000329","14431466516"])
c(8, "Executive summary 2; §3.3.1", "insight", "Six reviewers name the competitive wound precisely — rival habit trackers charge for the act of tracking habits: competitor_paywall_frustration 6 (6.59%, 5.000) — 'Finally a habit tracker app where you can add more than 5 habits for free!' (HR — names a competitor's 5-habit free cap); 'all the other apps like tell me I have to pay to put more things on the checklist' (SA); 'when we normalised that a habit tracker app, doesn't allow us to track habits? Fern demolishes this tendency' (UY); 'I have tried to use so many different habit trackers but felt so limited when having to pay for more habits' (AU) — unprompted from four continents: the category has trained users to expect a cap and removing it reads as generosity", "unlimited free habits vs rivals' caps", "purchase-driver", "6 (6.59%, 5.000)", "product-rule", "high-priority (n=6)", "generalisable", ["14305681444","14300463758","14166656933","14282000329"])
c(9, "Executive summary 3; §3.3.2 table (verbatim)", "positioning", "Displacement is the strongest quality signal because it is comparative: 35 (38.46%, all 5★) position the app against alternatives they tried — comparative_best 24 (26.37%), rivals overwhelming or complicated 8 (8.79%), rivals paywalled 6 (6.59%), long prior search 5 (5.49%): 'I have spent the last 3 years trying out all the different habit tracking apps but was never satisfied' (ZA); 'This is the best habit tracking app I've ever tried!!!(I've tried many)'; 'After years of looking for an app that has everything, yet is simple, I finally found Fern' (UY); 'I used finch before and it was really great when I was younger, but now it was just overwhelming and a bit annoying' (DE — the only competitor named); 'it's easier to use than other app that claims to be simple' (SA); 'unlike the other apps where you don't even know where the main functions are' (SA); comparative_best fell 17/45 (37.8%) → 7/46 (15.2%) between halves and 50% → 32% → 16% → 0% by month — a change in who reviews (category shoppers first), not competitive erosion; no review ranks the app below a competitor", "n/a", "praise", table("### 3.3.2 Competitive displacement", 0), "none", "high-priority", "generalisable", ["14423852732","14333652382","14308111831","14166656933","14385677429","14350090934","14467544215","14305573901"])
c(10, "Executive summary 4; §5.1 table (verbatim)", "monetization", "There is no monetisation to analyse — the central business risk: zero reviewers paid, subscribed, trialled, renewed or asked for a refund; payer categories all 0 (explicit purchase, trial, renewal/cancellation, refund, paywall encountered, strongly implied payer); a purchase-language audit across 91 returns 6 hits, all about the app not charging or about competitors ('Please dont put any subscription on it'; 'don't cost so much money for a subscription' of competitors; 'No hidden payments or subscriptions'); the only payment surface is a voluntary tip — 'there are no ads! But you can leave a tip, which is very nice ✨' (DE, 1 review) — 'complete evidence of product-market fit and zero evidence of revenue… not a finding that revenue is impossible'", "tip only", "none", table("## 5.1 There are no identifiable payers", 0), "research", "table", "generalisable", ["14245487733","14342658101","14355206933","14360994698","14401530018","14507313209","14277295245"])
c(11, "Executive summary 5; §5.2 table (verbatim)", "monetization", "Users pre-emptively defend the free model — the conversion window is narrow and emotionally charged: 8 (8.79%, 5.000) give a forward monetisation signal — plea_stay_free 4 (4.40%), willing_to_support 2 (2.20%), price_sensitivity_segment 4 (4.40%): 'Please dont put any subscription on it. This really helped my ADHD' (PH); 'I wish and hope and pray it will continue to be free and to those who can, please support the developers! <3' (PH — holds both positions); 'please be free forever 🙏' (PH); 'hope that it will continue its legacy by staying ✨FREE' (SA) — willingness to pay is patronage-shaped, not access-shaped: users will fund the developer, not be charged to track habits; introducing a habit cap would invert the theme that produced 57% of the praise", "free with tip", "praise", table("## 5.2 What the corpus says instead", 0), "product-rule", "table", "generalisable", ["14342658101","14410658467","14510494540","14305573901","14277295245"])
c(12, "Executive summary 6; §3.3.3 table (verbatim)", "product-rule", "Simplicity is the second product, defined by what the app refuses to do: UX praise 58 (63.74%, 5.000) — simplicity 35 (38.46%, 15 storefronts), ease of use 30 (32.97%, 13), aesthetic design 28 (30.77%, 18): 'Super simple and aesthetic… finally something that's not overcrowded and overwhelming!' (DE); 'beauty in simplicity… just pure habit tracking app' (UA); 'no visual noise, unlimited habits, great graphics' (UY); 'Its free and looks like an expensive one' (RO — design read as a price signal); 'doesn't have all the complicated tracking mechanics other apps have to get all caught up in' (US); 'Doesn't feel like a chore to set up or use' (ZA) — simplicity and aesthetics are separable (not the same reviewers), so visual richness without structural complexity can satisfy colour / theme requests without threatening the simplicity family", "minimal, aesthetic UI", "praise", table("### 3.3.3 UX praise", 0), "product-rule", "high-priority", "generalisable", ["14324333814","14361894176","14166656933","14319692985","14308111831","14467544215","14323412379"])
c(13, "Executive summary 7; §3.3.6 table (verbatim)", "audience", "The ADHD / neurodivergent segment is self-identifying, unprompted and near-unanimous: 8 (8.79%, 5.000) across 6 storefronts (US 3, CA, GB, PH, UA, ZA), 5 of 8 tying praise directly to simplicity — 'ADHD HEAVEN… Not overwhelming or complicated. Focused and easy. Doesn't feel like a chore to set up or use… my brain is happy' (ZA); 'Having a neurodivergent brain makes it so hard to start new habits. Setting up is simple. No extras' (US); 'i'm neurodivergent… simple but very attractive design, which is also a very needed condition for me… THE WIDGET that is clickable?? the best ever' (UA); 'As someone with adhd, I love that this is straight to the point'; 'Helped SO much with my ADHD'; the one dissent wants 'a bit more character and bit more of celebration for those who like dopamine hits due to neurodivergent' (GB) — the store positioning does not target ADHD; this segment found the product on its own: an under-claimed, high-conviction audience", "no ADHD positioning", "praise", table("### 3.3.6 The ADHD / neurodivergent segment", 0), "do", "high-priority (n=8)", "generalisable", ["14323412379","14285772400","14361894176","14401530018","14445278992","14342658101","14364392018","14406981588"])
c(14, "Executive summary 10; §3.4; §8.1 #1", "must-never-break", "A structural risk arrives on 1 January 2027: 'I noticed it only shows 2026 so I'm a bit nervous for when we go into the new year I hope all of my 2026 is not removed but time will tell!' (GB, 5★) — against 5 reviewers (5.49%) who chose the app specifically for unrestricted history ('you can see all of your history not just a month back'; 'see progress through a year'; 'look back on my weeks and months'); the corpus ends 118 days before the first year boundary — elevated above its 1.10% share under the severe-data-loss exception; fix 1: verify and publicise year-rollover behaviour, make prior-year history explicitly visible — 'the only dated, foreseeable risk in the report'", "history view shows only the current year", "complaint", "1 worry vs 5 adopted for history", "must-never-break", "severe-data-loss exception", "generalisable", ["14483338239","14360994698","14331162486","14333652382","14384650998","14328125592"])
c(15, "Executive summary 11; §4.4 table (verbatim)", "insight", "Both three-star reviews are requests, not complaints, and both are Saudi Arabian: one asks for more colours, emojis and a times-per-day frequency ('there isn't like enough colors and like I feel like the app is depressed… but overall it's free and you can customize and stuff but I'll keep the app as a daily use'); one asks only for cross-device use ('connect this great habit tracker to another device so I can work in two different places') — neither reports a bug, charge, data loss, crash or broken feature; one sits in the cosmetic-depth cluster and one in the structural cluster; SA's 4.200 mean (n=5) is a coincidence of a tiny sample, not a market finding", "n/a", "mixed", table("## 4.4 Three stars", 0), "none", "table", "app-specific", ["14348044475","14502988614"])
c(16, "Executive summary 12", "do", "The cheapest high-value moves in evidence order: confirm and publicise year-rollover behaviour before 1 Jan 2027 → decide the revenue model now while goodwill peaks, modelled on patronage framing rather than a cap → ship the icon/emoji and colour/theme expansion (the only repeat demand, and what a 3★ asks for) → add per-weekday and times-per-day frequency → fix the clear-mode widget icon rendering → scope cross-device sync (the only structural request) → diversify acquisition beyond TikTok → claim the ADHD / neurodivergent positioning", "n/a", "n/a", "report gives none", "do", "report recommendation", "generalisable", ["14410658467","14483065068","14348044475","14389332832","14281923322","14431546099","14333652382","14468763471","14502988614","14300350854"])

